    dev_i80321.c:446    d->enable = &cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;
    dev_i80321.c:84-87  { *d->status |= linemask;
                          if (*d->status & *d->enable) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); }

**`i80321_assert()` is a SECOND READER of the exact cell, outside the CP6 decode, and its observable is the ARM IRQ line.  That is clause (f)'s "side effect" anchor, verbatim** -- and it is reachable SYNCHRONOUSLY inside one guest store: `machine_iq80321.c:61-64` wires the ns16550 as `irq=...i80321.28`, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)` calls `dev_ns16550_tick()` inside the guest's own store for `com_ier` and `com_mcr`.  So `strb 0x02 -> 0xfe800001` then `strb 0x08 -> 0xfe800004` asserts line 28 and the `isrc & inten` test fires.

**THE COST, stated honestly: the anchor arm must FREE-RUN.**  `cpu_dyntrans.c:205-209` suppresses the exception under `single_step`, and `irq_asserted` is in neither the `reg` dump nor the settings tree, so a stepping arm cannot see it.  Use the project's existing free-running shape: clear CPSR I, plant `b .`, breakpoint at **0x18** (`ARM_EXCEPTION_IRQ = 6`, and `ARM_CONTROL_V` is not in the reset set), `continue`.  Contrast row: `inten = 0` gives no exception and the `b .` spins to the stall budget.

=== M1 -- A FLOOR BREACH (L2), AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION I MOST WANTED ===

    if (l_bit) { r[rd] = inten;
                 if (cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isrc != 0) { fatal(...); exit(1); } }

**`i80321_isrc` is 0 in every one of the six arms** -- nothing asserts an i80321 interrupt in a bare probe, and the only guest-reachable writer of `isrc` is the crn-8 MCR, which `exit(1)`s.  **On a real guest it is NEVER 0 at the moment INTCTL is read, because NetBSD reads INTCTL from inside the interrupt path.**  The host dies exactly as before and nothing in the six arms can see it.

Graded L2 rather than clause-(e) debt under the pass-11 generating-set ruling: `inten`'s ONLY consumer is `i80321_assert`/`i80321_deassert`, which reads `*d->status & *d->enable`, so `isrc` and `inten` are a JOINT INPUT of the model, written by different paths.  The floor is `{l_bit} x {inten classes} x {isrc = 0, != 0}` and **the whole `isrc != 0` column is empty.**  *The same anchor arm closes it: asserting the serial gives `isrc = 1<<28`, readable back through `MRC p6,0,Rt,c8,c0,0` = `0xEE187610` -- the crn-8 LOAD has no `exit(1)` (`:328-329`), a fact the design did not use.*

=== M5 -- A TOTAL ESCAPE, AND IT IS NOT A HOST KILL, WHICH MAKES IT WORSE ===

Swap the cell on BOTH sides to `i80321_isteer`: reset read 0, three round-trips, L, N, C, E and S all GREEN -- **while the device's interrupt enable mask is never written and a guest's interrupts silently never fire.**  The design's own anchor admission conceded this was invisible; with the channel above found, it is closable.

=== THREE MORE ESCAPES, EACH CLOSED BY ONE WORD ===
* **M2 -- the detector's real bound is FOUR READS PER PROCESS.**  Arm D performs exactly four subject reads and L performs one, each in a fresh process, so a `static int nread; if (++nread > 4) exit(1);` escapes 6/6 while a guest polling INTCTL dies.  Repair: one more read in arm D.
* **M3 -- the `rd` axis is not closed, and the manual says WHICH value matters.**  DDI 0406 A8.8.108: *"`<Rt>` ... can be R0-R14 or, in some cases, APSR_nzcv"* -- **Rt = R15 means write the CONDITION FLAGS**, and GXemul writes `r[15]`, the PC, unconditionally.  That is a real modelling defect, so a future `if (rd == ARM_PC) exit(1)` is likely.  `{3,4,5,6,9}` is five of sixteen and **omits the one value with an architectural special case**.  `0xEE10F610`.
* **M4 -- `opcode1`/`opcode2` are ignored by the arm and CONSTANT across all six invocations.**  `if (opcode2 != 0) exit(1)` escapes 6/6 and re-opens the kill for 7/8 of the encoding space.  Close with `0xEE10A630`.

=== CORRECTIONS TO THE DESIGN ===
* **A FOURTH TERMINATING SITE IS UNROWED**: crn 4 STORE with a non-zero value (`:320-325`).  It is the subject's nearest sibling inside the same `case crm 0:` switch, and under SIBLING PARITY crn 4 is uncovered in BOTH directions -- including the silent-accept read at `:317-318` that the disposition argument leans on.
* **L, N and C MERGE into one invocation** -- the rule binds only if the words must occupy the same address, and nothing requires that.  Six arms -> four, before the additions.
* **Arm E is strengthened for free**: `0xEE107612` yields three ZERO fields, so a broken extractor still mostly matches.  **`0xEE79B6B2`** gives `opcode1=3 opcode2=5 crn=9 crm=2 rd=11 l=1` -- six distinct non-trivial fields, turning E into a decode-field anchor at zero cost.
* **THE NOP WAS NAMED, NOT ENCODED.**  *"the NOP (`mov r0,r0`)"* is a LABEL; the word is `0xE1A00000`.  That is precisely this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap, recorded four times.
* **MY NON-INTERFERENCE REASON WAS WRONG AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES.**  I said the tick cannot fire inside a `step` because its input is a `timer_add` callback.  **The tick DOES fire** -- `machine.c:354` initialises `ticks_till_next[n] = 0` and `:727-730` fires at `<= 0`, so every tick function runs on step 1 of every invocation.  What protects the cell is the ELSE branch: `tmr0` is 0 at reset, so the else writes `i80321_isrc` and `tisr` and never `inten`.  *This matters for the free-running anchor arm, which must not assume the tick is inert -- that same step-1 tick calls `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` whenever `!(isrc & inten)`.*

